WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Why would someone lie about being sick or do terrible things to themselves in order to appear sick? Factitious disorder, more commonly known as Munchausen syndrome, is a mental health disorder in which people fake serious illness in order to gain sympathy, attention, and support. A related disorder, Munchausen by proxy, or a factitious disorder imposed on another, is a form of abuse in which caregivers, often mothers of young children, make up or even induce illness in their kids. This is to gain sympathy and support. How common are these disorders? Why do they go by so many different names?
为什么有人会假装生病，或者为了让自己看起来生病而对自己做出可怕的事情？虚假障碍，也就是俗称的孟乔森综合征，是一种心理健康疾病，患者会伪装成患有严重疾病，以获得同情、关注和支持。而与之相关的一种疾病是代理性孟乔森综合征，或者叫施加于他人的虚假障碍，这是一种虐待形式，照顾者（通常是幼儿的母亲）会捏造甚至诱发孩子的病情。这是为了获得同情和支持。这些疾病有多常见？为什么它们有这么多不同的名字？

2
00:00:41.000 --> 00:01:14.000
What's the difference between factitious disorder or Munchausen syndrome and simply malingering? Are factitious disorders related to other mental health disorders? Are there effective therapies? Can a person with factitious disorder be cured of it? And what about people who lie to strangers on the Internet about being sick? Is that a form of one of these disorders as well? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life.
虚假障碍或孟乔森综合征与单纯的诈病有什么区别？虚假障碍与其他心理健康疾病有关吗？有有效的治疗方法吗？患有虚假障碍的人能治愈吗？那些在互联网上向陌生人谎称自己生病的人呢？这也是其中一种疾病的形式吗？欢迎收听“心理学对话”（Speaking of Psychology），这是美国心理学协会的旗舰播客，探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。

3
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:56.000
I'm Kim Mills. We have two guests today. First is Dr. Mark Feldman, a board-certified psychiatrist, clinical professor of psychiatry, and adjunct professor of psychology at the University of Alabama. He is an international expert in factitious disorder, Munchausen syndrome, Munchausen by proxy, and Malingering. He has authored more than 100 research studies and five books on these topics, including his most recent book, Dying to Be Ill, True Stories of Medical Deception. Dr. Feldman is a distinguished fellow, of the American Psychiatric Association and has spoken to dozens of media outlets about Munchausen syndrome.
我是金·米尔斯（Kim Mills）。我们今天请到了两位嘉宾。首先是马克·费尔德曼博士（Dr. Mark Feldman），他是一位获得委员会认证的精神病学家、精神病学临床教授，也是阿拉巴马大学的心理学兼职教授。他是虚假障碍、孟乔森综合征、代理性孟乔森综合征和诈病领域的国际专家。他撰写了100多项研究报告和五本关于这些主题的书籍，其中包括他最新出版的书《渴望生病：医疗欺骗的真实故事》（Dying to Be Ill, True Stories of Medical Deception）。费尔德曼博士是美国精神病学协会的杰出资深会员，曾就孟乔森综合征接受过数十家媒体的采访。

4
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Our second guest is Dr. Janet Cahill, a clinical psychologist and professor emerita in the Department of Psychology at Rowan University. Her private practice specializes in assessing abused and neglected children and their families, including evaluations and treatments for factitious disorder imposed on another, or Munchausen by proxy. Dr. Cahill has consulted with many human services agencies, including the New Jersey Division of Child Permanency and Protection, the Nevada Division of Child and Family Services, and the South Carolina and Colorado Departments of Child Protective Services. She also provides workshops and training to human services professionals. Thank you both for joining me today. It's a pleasure.
我们的第二位嘉宾是珍妮特·卡希尔博士（Dr. Janet Cahill），她是一位临床心理学家，也是罗文大学心理学系的荣誉退休教授。她的私人诊所专门评估受虐待和忽视的儿童及其家庭，包括对施加于他人的虚假障碍（即代理性孟乔森综合征）的评估和治疗。卡希尔博士曾为许多人类服务机构提供咨询，包括新泽西州儿童永久安置与保护局、内华达州儿童与家庭服务局以及南卡罗来纳州和科罗拉多州的儿童保护服务部门。她还为人类服务专业人员提供研讨会和培训。非常感谢两位今天做客节目。我的荣幸。

5
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Thank you. In the introduction, I mentioned that these disorders are known by several different names. Factitious disorder is the official term that's used in the diagnostic and statistical manual of mental disorders, but most people, if they've heard of this disorder, are more familiar with the term Munchausen syndrome. And factitious disorder imposed on another, or Munchausen by proxy, is also sometimes called medical child abuse. Are these terms basically interchangeable? What's the history of these terms? And which do you use for which? Dr. Feldman, maybe you want to grab this one first. Yeah, there's been a lot of complexity because so many terms have been used to refer somewhat to munchausen syndrome, that is, people making themselves sick or lying a bad illness, but really to munchausen by proxy, which I do prefer to call medical child abuse. And the reason I prefer that is it focuses on the fact that this is abuse.
谢谢。在介绍中，我提到这些疾病有几个不同的名字。虚假障碍是《精神疾病诊断与统计手册》中使用的官方术语，但大多数人如果听说过这种疾病，对“孟乔森综合征”这个词会更熟悉。而施加于他人的虚假障碍（或代理性孟乔森综合征）有时也被称为医疗儿童虐待。这些术语基本上可以互换吗？这些术语的历史是什么？你分别用哪个词来指代什么？费尔德曼博士，也许你想先回答这个问题。是的，这里面有很多复杂性，因为有太多的术语被用来多多少少地指代孟乔森综合征，也就是人们让自己生病或谎称自己得了重病，但实际上是指代理性孟乔森综合征，我更倾向于称之为医疗儿童虐待。我更喜欢这个词的原因是它聚焦于这是一个虐待事实的事实。

6
00:03:42.000 --> 00:04:32.000
It typically involves a child being victimized. And it's easier to understand. Munchesom by proxy doesn't tell you very much about what the ailment is and what the behavior is, whereas medical child abuse makes it clear, and it also helps focus on the child victim rather than the adult perpetrator, who's usually the mother. But formally, the DSM-5 and DSM-5TR do call these factitious disorder imposed on self in the case of what we used to call Munchausen syndrome. And as you said, factitious disorder imposed on another for what people still tend to know is Munchausen by proxy. Dr. Cahill, does that resonate with you?
它通常涉及儿童沦为受害者。而且它更容易理解。代理性孟乔森综合征并没有告诉你太多关于病情和行为是什么的信息，而医疗儿童虐待则使其一目了然，它还有助于将重点放在儿童受害者身上，而不是通常是母亲的成年施暴者身上。但从形式上讲，DSM-5和DSM-5-TR确实将我们以前称之为孟乔森综合征的情况称为施加于自身的虚假障碍。正如你所说，对于人们仍然倾向于了解的代理性孟乔森综合征，则称为施加于他人的虚假障碍。卡希尔博士，这与你的看法产生共鸣吗？

7
00:04:32.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Do you also use factitious disorder rather than Munchausen? So as a psychologist, I use factitious disorder imposed on another. And so that allows us to make that diagnosis. We're really looking at the same stuff, at the same symptoms, at the same problems. But our different professional needs are, if we're going to do it, we need to do it within our own areas. So I would use factitious disorder imposed on another. But honestly, for the most case, we're all looking at the same thing. Well, let me ask you the different.
你也用虚假障碍而不是孟乔森综合征吗？作为一名心理学家，我使用施加于他人的虚假障碍。这使我们能够做出这种诊断。我们实际上是在看同样的东西，同样的症状，同样的问题。但我们的不同专业需求是，如果我们要这样做，我们需要在自己的领域内完成。所以我会使用施加于他人的虚假障碍。但老实说，在大多数情况下，我们大家看到的都是同一件事。好吧，让我问你不同的地方。

8
00:05:05.000 --> 00:06:31.000
between factitious disorders and simply malingering, which is basically faking or exaggerating illness, are there cases where people pretend to be sick but they don't have factitious disorder? Yeah, this can occur in, well, let me clarify the difference between factitious disorder and malingering, but also point out that they can coexist. Malingering involves external gains, primarily going after things that are tangible, like feigning or exaggerating illness or even inducing illness to get opioid medications or disability adjudications or not guilty by reason of insanity adjudications or evasion of military service. It's things that when you look at the patient situation, you can sort of understand why they've done what they've done. Whereas in factitious disorders in general, you have to infer psychological processes because they're after things like that aren't tangible, like intention, sympathy, care, and concern that they feel unable to get in other ways. But as I said, we all know motivations can shift over time. They can even coexist.
虚假障碍与单纯的诈病（基本上就是伪装或夸大病情）之间有什么区别？有没有这样一种情况：人们假装生病，但他们并没有患虚假障碍？是的，这种情况可能会发生，让我澄清一下虚假障碍和诈病之间的区别，但也指出它们可以共存。诈病涉及外部获得，主要是追求有形的东西，比如伪装、夸大病情甚至诱发疾病来获得阿片类药物药物、残志鉴定、因精神失常而无罪的裁决或逃避兵役。当你审视患者的情况时，你会发现这些事情是你可以理解他们为什么这样做的。而在虚假障碍中，总的来说，你必须推断心理过程，因为他们追求的是无形的东西，比如关注、同情、照顾和关心，而他们觉得无法通过其他方式获得这些。但正如我所说，我们都知道动机随着时间推移会发生变化。它们甚至可以共存。

9
00:06:31.000 --> 00:07:23.000
And so I never hesitate to make the diagnosis of both if that's what's applicable in a given case. Dr. Cahill, let me ask you, how common do you think these disorders are? Do we have any reliable estimates? Well, by definition, an individual who meets the criteria for a factitious disorder is doing this on purpose for their own psychological needs. And so it's very, very hard to catch up with them. We have colleagues now who are doing some research on this. And the more we research it, the more we find it. So I think it's out there more than we expected, but it's also difficult to get a handle on it. I would just add that the recognition also has occurred that munchausen by proxy or factitious disorder imposed on another can also coexist with munchausen syndrome.
因此，如果特定病例适用的话，我绝不会犹豫同时做出这两种诊断。卡希尔博士，请问你认为这些疾病有多常见？我们有什么可靠的估计吗？嗯，根据定义，符合虚假障碍标准的个人是为了满足自己的心理需求而故意这样做的。因此，要追查到他们非常非常困难。我们现在有一些同行正在对此进行研究。我们研究得越多，发现的就越多。所以我认为这种情况比我们预期的要普遍，但也很难掌握其确切情况。我只想补充一点，人们也认识到，代理性孟乔森综合征或施加于他人的虚假障碍也可以与孟乔森综合征共存。

10
00:07:23.000 --> 00:08:10.000
so somebody can be feigning illness in themselves and do it in a child as well. Sometimes we find that, and research has borne this out, up to a third of abusive moms in Munchesom biproxy cases have a personal history of feigning illness in themselves. And this is deliberate deception. They know what they're doing. There's often considerable planning involved in executing the behaviors that. deceptive behaviors. And so anyone who says, well, DSM contains these two diagnoses, the mothers can't, and patients can't be held responsible. No, this is deliberate behavior. And I always point out that
因此，一个人可能会在自己身上伪装疾病，同时也对孩子这样做。有时我们发现，研究也证实了这一点，在代理性孟乔森综合征病例中，多达三分之一的施虐母亲有在自己身上伪装疾病的个人历史。这是蓄意欺骗。他们知道自己在做什么。执行这些欺诈行为往往涉及相当多的策划。因此，任何人如果说，嗯，DSM包含了这两种诊断，母亲们不能，患者也不能承担责任。不，这是蓄意的行为。我总是指出，

11
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:47.000
pedophilia is also a mental disorder. But we certainly prosecute those who sexually abuse children. And sometimes we have to seriously look at prosecution in the Munchausen cases. especially medical child abuse or munchausen by proxy? It would seem that for someone to manifest either version of factitious disorders, that there must have been some kind of trauma, something happened in a person's life that led them to this behavior. One would think, how much is known about the causes of factitious disorder? Are there common threads?
恋童癖也是一种精神疾病。但我们肯定会起诉那些性虐待儿童的人。有时我们必须认真考虑在孟乔森综合征的案件中进行起诉。尤其是医疗儿童虐待或代理性孟乔森综合征？似乎对于表现出任何一种虚假障碍的人来说，一定经历过某种创伤，即一个人的生活中发生了某种事情，导致了他们这种行为。人们可能会想，人们对虚假障碍的原因了解多少？有什么共同的线索吗？

12
00:08:47.000 --> 00:09:10.000
As Mark was saying, a lot of times they did this, they might have been the victims of that kind of behavior. They might have been exposed to it. So that's possibility. It might be trauma. But generally speaking, it's a psychological need that this parent has. Maybe it could be attention behavior. Maybe it could be they want to be seen as a fantastic mom.
正如马克所说，很多时候他们这样做，他们可能是这种行为的受害者。他们可能接触过这种行为。所以这是一种可能性。可能是创伤。但总的来说，这是这位父母的一种心理需求。也许可能是寻求关注的行为。也许可能是他们希望被视为一个了不起的母亲。

13
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:43.000
Maybe they have issues of control. They want to control everyone around them. So there's multiple paths to this. But a general way to think about it is this is a parent, and Mark is correct, it's almost all moms who are trying to get to, who are manipulating this child to meet their own psychological needs, whatever those are. Dr. Feldman, let me ask you, how difficult is it to diagnose factitious disorders? I mean, do you think that MDs are sufficiently aware of the syndromes so that they can spot it in patients?
也许他们有控制欲方面的问题。他们想控制周围的每个人。所以通往这条路的途径有很多。但总的来说，这是一个父母——马克说得对，几乎全都是母亲——试图通过操纵这个孩子来满足她们自己的心理需求，无论这些需求是什么。费尔德曼博士，请问诊断虚假障碍有多困难？我的意思是，你认为医学博士对这些综合征有足够的认识，以便在患者身上发现它吗？

14
00:09:43.000 --> 00:10:41.000
And if people are doctor shopping, which often these people will do, can they go undetected for, long periods of time. I actually believe that the majority of these cases go undetected. And a lot of that has to do with a knowledge deficit and an educational deficit among health care providers. It's actually getting better. But I, for example, did my medical school training at Dartmouth and my residency training at Duke. And I never heard the term. factitious or any discussion of munchesom by proxy throughout my education. The first time I heard of it was when I was confronted with a patient in my office who had feigned terminal breast cancer for a year and a half and I suddenly had to do a lot of research on my own to figure out what was happening.
如果人们在“逛医院”（通常这些人会这样做），他们能长时间不被发现吗？我实际上相信大多数这类病例都没有被发现。这在很大程度上与医疗保健提供者的知识缺陷和教育缺陷有关。情况实际上正在好转。但例如，我在达特茅斯完成了医学院培训，在杜克大学完成了住院医师培训。在我的整个教育过程中，我从未听过“虚假”这个词，也没有听到过关于代理性孟乔森综合征的任何讨论。我第一次听说这件事，是在我的办公室里遇到了一位患者，她伪装了晚期乳腺癌一年半，我突然不得不自己做很多研究来弄清楚发生了什么。

15
00:10:41.000 --> 00:11:30.000
She did have a full-blown case of factitious disorder imposed on self. We did call it just plain factitious disorder at the time. But if a patient is particularly crafty, we may see no red flags. And again, as I said before, I think the majority of these cases go unrecognized. So how can an expert differentiate between malingering and hypochondria? I mean, they sound very similar. Are these disorders connected? Are they the same thing? Dr. Cahill? Any thoughts on that? Yes. We do have, as a profession, we've gotten better in our ability to do evaluations that separate those things out.
她确实患有典型的施加于自身的虚假障碍症。当时我们只是称之为单纯的虚假障碍。但如果患者特别狡猾，我们可能看不到任何危险信号。而且，正如我之前所说，我认为大多数这类病例都没有被识别出来。那么，专家如何区分诈病和恐病症呢？我的意思是，它们听起来非常相似。这些疾病有联系吗？它们是同一回事吗？卡希尔博士？对此有什么看法吗？是的。作为一个专业群体，我们在进行能够将这些事情区分开来的评估能力方面有所提高。

16
00:11:30.000 --> 00:12:10.000
So if we have enough information, we can make a distinction between somebody who really feels like their child is having this problem. I'm usually coming from the child perspective, but Mark is right. This can also be just an individual with factitious disorder. So what we need then is we need to get tons of records, and we need to get lots and lots of information, and we have to compare what the potential perpetrator is saying versus what the records are doing. And if we're careful about that and we get enough of the records and we have enough time, we can make the distinction between someone who just is really concerned about their child and messing up to someone who is doing this on purpose.
因此，如果我们有足够的信息，我们就能区分出那些真正觉得自己的孩子有这个问题的人。我通常从儿童的角度出发，但马克是对的。这也可能只是一个患有虚假障碍的个人。所以我们需要的是，我们需要获取大量的记录，我们需要获得许许多多的信息，我们必须比较潜在施害者所说的话与记录所显示的内容。如果我们对此保持谨慎，并获得足够的记录并且有足够的时间，我们就能区分出那些真正关心孩子并把事情搞砸的人与那些故意这样做的人。

17
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:58.000
So, for example, sometimes if a nurse has a concern, they'll put a camera in the room where the mom is with the child, and we've had a number of these. So you have to get a court agreement for that. You just can't start putting cameras in the hospitals. But let's say we have a camera in the hospital with a mom who says that her child keeps throwing up and he's vomiting and he can't focus anymore. So we look at that after a while and we see that the child is not focusing, but literally the mom is putting a pillow over the case. kid's head and blocking it happens. So there we go. We're set. We know what we're doing. We saw it with our own eyes. Even with that, they will deny to the end of time. It's very difficult to get them to admit they did this. But we have now better protocols where by using the information
所以，举个例子，有时如果护士有担忧，他们会在妈妈和孩子呆的房间里放一个摄像头，我们已经处理过好几个这样的案例了。所以你必须为此获得法院的同意。你不能随随便便就开始在医院里安装摄像头。但假设我们在医院里安装了一个摄像头，里面有一个妈妈，她说她的孩子一直在呕吐，而且他无法集中注意力了。所以过了一会儿我们看了看，发现孩子确实无法集中注意力，但实际上是妈妈把一个枕头放在孩子的头上并捂住——这种情况是会发生的。所以我们就抓到了。我们准备好了。我们知道自己在做什么。我们亲眼目睹了这一切。即便如此，他们也会矢口否认，直到永远。要让他们承认自己做了这件事是非常困难的。但我们现在有了更好的协议，通过利用

18
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:45.000
we have, it doesn't have to be a camera, but it could be a lot of records, having someone, just a quick example, said her kid keeps throwing up when she goes to school all the time. Talk to the teacher who's there to guard the kids at the school going across the street, she said the kid never throws up. So if we can build a pattern of that, it can't just be one thing, we can make that diagnosis with some accuracy. Dr. Feldman, I want to ask you a question. Back in the 1990s, you coined the term Munchausen by Internet. How has the Internet changed how this disorder presents itself? And is Munchausen by Internet as common as regular Munchausen syndrome, or is it even more common at this point. Well, I coined the term because people, when I lecture it, would often come up to me
我们掌握的信息——不一定非得是摄像头，也可以是大量的记录。举个简单的例子，有人说她的孩子每次去学校时都一直在吐。去问问那个在学校路口护送孩子过马路的老师，她说那个孩子从来不吐。因此，如果我们能够建立这样一个规律，而不能仅仅依靠一件事，我们就可以相当准确地做出诊断。费尔德曼博士，我想问你一个问题。早在20世纪90年代，你创造了“网络孟乔森综合征”（Munchausen by Internet）这个词。互联网是如何改变这种疾病的表现形式的？网络孟乔森综合征像常规的孟乔森综合征那样常见，还是说在这一点上它甚至更常见？嗯，我创造这个词是因为当我做讲座时，人们经常会走到我面前

